/*
 * Spoken syllables, base64 MP3.
 *
 * Rendered locally by tools/render-syllables.py with Piper (MIT), a neural
 * text-to-speech engine, and its LibriTTS "high" English voice, speaker 0
 * (LibriTTS reader 3922). The voice is trained on LibriTTS (Zen et al. 2019,
 * openslr.org/60), licensed CC BY 4.0, which is the attribution owed here.
 * It replaced flite's CMU slt voice, which was clear but plainly synthetic.
 * Re-run that script rather than editing this file by hand.
 *
 * A DESIGNED INVENTORY, NOT A LIST OF WORDS. Seven onsets, seven vowels and
 * seven codas, each set spread as far across its part of the phoneme space as
 * English allows; every onset meets every vowel, and the coda is fixed by that
 * pair. Forty-nine nonsense syllables, of which no two differ in only one
 * sound — a property of the construction rather than of anyone checking.
 *
 * Nonsense rather than words (the set before this one was bark, chime, dune,
 * ...) because a word is remembered as a word. Lexical items come with
 * meaning, frequency and associations, all of which give a listener a second
 * route to "did I hear this recently" that has nothing to do with the phonology
 * the channel is meant to be testing — and sixteen of them is a small enough
 * set that the route is worth taking.
 *
 * They are closed syllables (consonant-vowel-consonant) rendered a little
 * slower than the voice's default because an open syllable at a TTS voice's own rate
 * lasts a fifth of a second, which is over before attention has arrived at it,
 * and a listener who missed the stimulus is not being tested on memory. A coda
 * roughly doubles the clip and gives the ear a second landmark to identify the
 * item by. The speed is not baked in past this baseline — syllableVoice.ts
 * stretches these clips at playback time, so the rate is a setting rather than
 * a re-render.
 *
 * Forty-nine rather than eight because the set size is what makes a syllable
 * worth holding: with eight, a third of the non-matches inside any n-window
 * repeat something recent by accident, and the channel drifts towards
 * familiarity rather than recall. How many of them are actually in play is the
 * variety setting; the list is ordered so that any leading slice of it is a
 * spread of the whole inventory rather than one corner of it.
 *
 * The first version of this set was four deliberate minimal pairs (bah/pah,
 * dah/tah, gah/kah, mah/nah), built to supply an acoustically confusable lure.
 * That model was wrong: a lure has to be falsely *recalled* as a match, and two
 * syllables sounding alike does not make them confusable in memory — you either
 * encoded the syllable or you did not. The lure is temporal now (see the
 * syllable case in NBackGame), so confusability between the stimuli themselves
 * is no longer wanted, and that is why the coda is tied to the onset and vowel:
 * it keeps single-phoneme pairs out of the inventory entirely.
 *
 * Base64 rather than files in public/: a lure only works if it arrives on time,
 * and a fetch that has not landed when the trial fires is a trial with no
 * stimulus in it. The whole set is a couple of hundred kilobytes, decoded once
 * at the start of a session, which is cheaper than that risk.
 */

